{"entries":{"chapter-89-study-breast-disease|Chapter 89 Study: Breast Disease":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Breast cancer incidence increases with age; US lifetime diagnosis risk is 12.8% in the chapter's cited data (p1395).":["u3"],"copy|Older patients can benefit from endocrine therapy and chemotherapy as much as younger patients, but chemotherapy toxicity may be greater (p1395).":["u4"],"copy|Use geriatric assessment and estimated life expectancy apart from breast cancer to guide treatment choices (p1395).":["u5"],"copy|A new palpable breast mass after menopause needs prompt investigation and biopsy; mammography can characterize the mass and identify other lesions (p1397).":["u6"],"copy|After diagnosis, perform receptor testing and staging to define local and distant extent; stage and receptor status drive treatment (p1399).":["u7"],"copy|Breast conservation removes the tumor with clear margins, assesses axillary nodes when indicated and often includes radiotherapy; survival is comparable to more extensive surgery (p1400).":["u8"],"copy|Separate-quadrant tumors, diffuse suspicious calcifications, prior chest radiotherapy or persistently positive margins may preclude conservation (p1400).":["u9"],"copy|Large tumor-to-breast ratio, illustrated by a mass above 5 cm, can compromise cosmesis; neoadjuvant treatment may make conservation possible in selected patients (p1400).":["u10"],"copy|After mastectomy, radiotherapy is generally recommended for tumors above 5 cm or at least 4 positive nodes; the chapter also considers it reasonable with nodal involvement (p1400).":["u11"],"copy|Endocrine-refractory or triple-negative disease generally leads to chemotherapy; life-threatening or rapidly progressive visceral disease may also require it initially (p1403).":["u12"],"copy|Do not withhold chemotherapy solely because of age; consider tumor extent, symptoms, expected benefit and toxicity (p1403).":["u13"],"copy|The authors reserve preventive drug consideration for a few high-risk older patients with at least 10 years estimated life expectancy (p1405).":["u15"],"copy|More favorable competing-risk estimates do not make breast cancer harmless; higher-risk tumors remain lethal for many older patients (p1395).":["u16"],"copy|Incorporate transport, family support, cost and access into treatment plans. Initiate palliative care early for almost all symptomatic patients (p1406).":["u17"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u18"],"table-89-4-book-image|Table 89-4 — book image":["u19"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 89-4, p.1404":["u20","u34"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u21"],"copy|CHAPTER 89s / HAZZARD":["u24"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 89 - Breast Disease; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u25"],"copy|Disease & initial assessment":["u26"],"copy|Local treatment":["u27"],"copy|Systemic treatment & supportive care":["u28"],"copy|Exam traps":["u29"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u30"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u31"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u33"],"copy|Four past-exam questions are linked to this chapter.":["u33"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Assess left ventricular function before trastuzumab and at intervals during therapy.":["u191"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Multiple births are generally associated with lower long-term risk, although risk can rise temporarily after childbirth.":["u192"],"copy|The chapter suggests considering annual or biennial screening if estimated life expectancy is at least ten years and discussing the limited likely benefit when it is five to ten years (pp1397–1398, Table 89-1).":["u86"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The USPSTF found the evidence insufficient at age 75 and older both in the guidance available in 2020 and in its 2024 update.":["u87"],"copy|Tamoxifen is the preferred adjuvant endocrine agent for men with hormone-sensitive disease (pp1405–1406).":["u181"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): An aromatase inhibitor with gonadal suppression is a possible alternative when tamoxifen is contraindicated.":["u182"],"copy|CHAPTER 89 / HAZZARD":["u24"],"copy|Prevention with estrogen-directed drugs can reduce breast-cancer occurrence, but the prevention trials did not show a survival advantage (p1405).":["u14"],"copy|September 2024 Q41":["u22"],"copy|June 2025 Q67":["u23","u32"]},"maxId":192,"sequenceOffsets":[[193,-158],[214,-157],[229,-156],[242,-153],[334,-150],[341,0]]}
